<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#repartimiento">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Repartimiento</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The repartimiento ('distribution') was a colonial labour system imposed on the Indigenous peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, under which Amerindians of the pueblos de indios were drafted to work on farms, in mines, in workshops and on public projects. Instated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-laws">New Laws of 1542</a> to replace the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a>, it was not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>: workers were not owned, were paid wages and served in turns, with only 7-10% of the adult men to be assigned at once. Yet it created slavery-like conditions in places, most notoriously in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines of 16th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">mita</a>. As Indigenous populations collapsed and workers left their pueblos, wage labour came to dominate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Indigenous communities nevertheless survived as self-governing towns that paid tribute and supplied labour, first through the encomienda and later the <b>repartimiento</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In New Spain the <b>repartimiento</b> drafted smaller numbers for a few weeks a year, for a wage.</blockquote><a href="/societies/spanish-america">Social Hierarchy of Spanish America</a></li></ul></section>
